# ADR-P041 supply-chain gate — the BOUNDED, rc-ONLY escape hatch (issues #354, #628; ADR-P041 v1.8).
#
# For exactly one situation: a FIXABLE CRITICAL/HIGH lands in the base image, the fixed base is not
# published upstream yet (or not yet built and smoke-tested), and a prerelease must still ship.
#
# It is a DELAY, never a waiver. Since ADR-P041 v1.10 the Dockerfile's `apt-get upgrade` layer
# applies Debian's fixes at build time, so a fixable base CVE should not reach `verify` at all. An
# entry here is now for the residue: a fix Debian has not yet published to the security archive the
# build reads, or a finding outside the Debian packages.
#
# RULES:
#   1. Every entry carries `exp:YYYY-MM-DD`. Trivy itself stops honouring the line on that date,
#      so a forgotten suppression re-reddens `verify` instead of quietly becoming permanent.
#   2. The expiry is at most `_MAX_DAYS` out (7), inclusive. Mechanised by
#      tests/test_trivyignore_policy.py, a form check that cannot see the channel, so the cap is
#      the same for every build.
#   3. A `# why:` line naming a tracking issue sits directly above the entry (same test).
#   4. PRERELEASE ONLY. On a GA tag, `verify` fails while ANY line here is active
#      (scripts/trivyignore_channel_gate.py). GA's remedy is a build that carries the fix: the
#      `apt-get upgrade` layer for a Debian package, otherwise a base bump. Then delete the line.
#   5. Every active entry is printed into the `verify` job summary on EVERY run, so a live bypass
#      is visible to anyone reading a build — not only to someone who thinks to open this file.
#
# While an entry is live, sandbox-base-image-watch.yml scans with this same file AND alarms on the
# active line itself (`release_clean_under_suppression`, ADR-P041 v1.9), and `make ga` refuses.
#
# Format:
#   # why: <reason>; base bump tracked in #NNN
#   CVE-YYYY-NNNNN exp:YYYY-MM-DD
